---
import JapanServicePage from "@components/japan/JapanServicePage.astro";
import type { Article } from "@components/japan/JapanServicePage.astro";
import { createPageSeo } from "@lib/seo/seo-config";
import { createBreadcrumbs } from "@lib/breadcrumbs/breadcrumbs";
import { usd, countryOgImage } from "@data/countries/international";
import { JPY_PER_USD, jpy, both, ja, japanRegions, japanServiceSchema } from "@data/countries/japan";

const path = "/japan/software-development/";

// ---------- FAQ (visible list and FAQPage schema share this array; plain text only) ----------

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "How much does custom software development cost in Japan?",
    answer: `2026 price guides from Japanese comparison sites put a small web system at about ¥500,000 to ¥2,000,000, a mid-size system at ¥2,000,000 to ¥5,000,000 and a full business system at ¥5,000,000 to ¥10,000,000 or more. Booking and membership systems are often quoted at ¥3,000,000 to ¥5,000,000. Our custom web apps start from ${both("Custom web app")} for a clearly scoped first version, and AI automation starts from ${both("AI automation")}. The final quote depends on screens, roles, integrations and data migration, and we itemise it in writing before any work starts.`,
  },
  {
    question: "Why are your prices lower than Japanese system development companies?",
    answer: "Japanese vendors carry Japanese costs: office rent, salaries for sales staff, project managers and engineers, and on larger projects a chain of subcontractors in which each layer adds a margin. We are three engineers in India working remotely, with no office, no sales team and no subcontractors, so the people who scope the work are the people who write the code. The trade-offs are real. We work in English, we cannot visit your site, and we are not certified under ISO 27001, so some procurement rules may exclude us.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do you have an office or staff in Japan?",
    answer: "No. BtechWaleTech has no office, business registration, staff or address in Japan, and we do not use a virtual office to suggest otherwise. We are three engineers based in India: Ankur Kumar for full stack development, Santosh Sharma for AI, AWS, data and deployment, and Vedansh Shrivastava for project delivery, data science and automation. All work is remote, over WhatsApp, video calls, email and a shared staging environment. If your project needs on-site work in Japan, a local vendor is the better choice.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can you work from Japanese requirements documents?",
    answer: "We work in English and do not read or write natural Japanese, so we cannot work directly from a long Japanese specification. What works well is a short English requirements document that we draft with you, with Japanese screen labels, field names and messages kept in a separate sheet that your bilingual colleague or translator maintains. Machine translation helps us understand existing Japanese documents, but anything that decides behaviour, such as a calculation rule or an approval flow, is confirmed in English in writing before we build it.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do you work under ukeoi (fixed deliverable) or jun-inin (time-based) contracts?",
    answer: "Both patterns are possible. New builds usually work like an ukeoi contract: a written scope, a fixed price, milestone payments and a defined deliverable that you accept after testing. Ongoing work such as maintenance, small improvements and exploratory phases works more like jun-inin, where we provide agreed effort or a monthly service rather than promising a specific result. We do not supply engineers under an SES arrangement to work under your direct instructions. Which contract form suits your company, and its legal wording, is for your own adviser to confirm.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do we need a bridge SE for an offshore project?",
    answer: "Large offshore projects often use a bridge SE, a bilingual engineer who translates requirements and manages the offshore team. On a small or mid-sized project that role is usually too expensive, and it adds a layer between you and the developers. What we ask for instead is one person on your side who can read and write English, even at a basic level, to approve the scope and relay feedback. We keep messages short, use screenshots and staging links, and write every decision down so it translates cleanly.",
  },
  {
    question: "How do you handle requirements definition?",
    answer: "We run a lighter version of the Japanese requirements definition phase. In one or two calls we map who uses the system, what they do today, which screens and reports they need, what data exists and which systems it must connect to. We then write a scope document listing features, user roles, integrations, data migration, exclusions and acceptance criteria, with rough screen sketches. You approve it before development starts, and changes after approval are quoted separately so the budget never drifts without you knowing.",
  },
  {
    question: "How does acceptance testing work, and who fixes defects after delivery?",
    answer: "Before handover we test against the acceptance criteria in the scope, and we write a test specification listing the main cases and their expected results, which fits Japanese acceptance (kenshu) routines. Your team then tests on the staging environment for an agreed period and signs off. Anything that does not match the agreed scope is fixed at no charge, and after launch you get five months of free maintenance. How long formal liability for non-conformity lasts should be written into the contract and checked by your adviser.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can you integrate with kintone, freee and Money Forward Cloud?",
    answer: `Yes. kintone, freee and Money Forward Cloud publish APIs for their main products, as do Salesforce, Slack, Chatwork, LINE, Shopify, Stripe, Google Workspace and Microsoft 365. Typical integrations create invoices or journal entries from paid orders, sync customer records between kintone and a web portal, post alerts to Chatwork or Slack, and send LINE messages at key steps. Each integration runs under an account your company owns, with the minimum permissions needed. Simple flows start from ${both("AI automation")}; larger integrations are part of a custom build.`,
  },
  {
    question: "Can you replace our Excel sheets or an outgrown kintone app?",
    answer: "Yes, and we usually start by keeping what works. Many Japanese teams run sales, stock or project data in Excel files shared by email, or in kintone apps that became slow and fragile as lookups and plugins piled up. We map the data and the daily routine, then build a web system with proper tables, user roles, change history and exports in the Excel format staff already use. Sometimes the best answer is to keep kintone as the entry screen and move only the heavy processing or reporting into a custom service.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can you modernise an old in-house system?",
    answer: "Often, yes. Common cases are Access databases, Excel VBA tools, old PHP or Java web systems and on-premises servers nearing the end of support. We first document what the system actually does, including rules nobody wrote down, then rebuild it in stages while the old system keeps running. Japanese legacy data brings its own work: Shift_JIS or CP932 text converted to UTF-8, machine-dependent characters, half-width katakana, Japanese era dates and custom kanji in names. We rehearse the data migration before the final switch-over.",
  },
  {
    question: "Where will our system and data be hosted?",
    answer: "Usually in a Japanese cloud region. AWS, Google Cloud and Microsoft Azure all operate regions in Tokyo and Osaka, so a common design runs production in Tokyo with backups or a standby copy in Osaka for disaster recovery. The cloud account is opened in your company's name and billed to you directly, which keeps the data and access under your control. If your internal policy requires data to stay in Japan, we design for that and document which service stores what.",
  },
  {
    question: "Are you ISO 27001 certified?",
    answer: "No. We do not hold ISO 27001, the ISMS certification many Japanese companies ask vendors about, the Privacy Mark or any other security certification. What we do in practice: least-privilege access, multi-factor authentication on every account, code review, dependency and security updates, separate staging and production environments, encrypted backups, secrets kept out of source code, and access removed at the end of a project. We answer security check sheets honestly. If certification is mandatory under your procurement rules, we are not the right vendor.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can your team in India access our customers' personal data?",
    answer: "Only when it is genuinely necessary. Under Japan's Act on the Protection of Personal Information, letting a contractor in a foreign country access personal data counts as providing it to a third party in a foreign country, which generally needs either the individual's informed consent or a contractor with an equivalent protection system. India's Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023 is being phased in. We build and test with dummy data, keep production access minimal and sign data-handling terms. Your adviser should decide the legal basis; this is not legal advice.",
  },
  {
    question: "Do you add Japanese consumption tax or issue qualified invoices?",
    answer: "No. We are an India-based provider and not registered as a qualified invoice issuer in Japan, so our invoices do not add Japanese consumption tax and are not qualified invoices under the invoice system. How the service is treated for consumption tax, and whether any withholding tax applies, depends on the type of service and your company's situation, so please ask your tax accountant (zeirishi) before the first payment. We are also not a registered IT support provider, so our work is not eligible for IT adoption subsidies.",
  },
  {
    question: "Can you invoice in yen, and how do we pay?",
    answer: `Yes. Our prices are published in US dollars, with approximate yen at ¥${JPY_PER_USD} per dollar for comparison; in September 2026 the rate moved between about ¥153 and ¥157. On request we fix a JPY amount in the written quote, so exchange movements do not change what you owe. Payment is by Wise or international bank wire, in milestones: an advance to start, a payment at design approval and the balance before launch. Cloud, API and licence costs are paid by you directly to those providers.`,
  },
  {
    question: "Who owns the source code and intellectual property?",
    answer: "You do, once the invoices for the work are paid. The source code lives in a repository under your company's account, and cloud, domain, database and third-party service accounts are in your company's name from the start. At handover you receive every login, the deployment instructions and English documentation of the architecture, data model and integrations. Open-source libraries keep their own licences, which we list. There is no lock-in: you can move maintenance to another vendor or an in-house team whenever you like.",
  },
  {
    question: "What hours are you available in Japan time?",
    answer: "Japan is three and a half hours ahead of India, and neither country changes its clocks for daylight saving. We work 9 am to 9 pm India time, which is 12:30 pm to 12:30 am in Japan, seven days a week on WhatsApp. Calls fit well into the Japanese afternoon, and a morning call around 10 am JST can be arranged in advance, for example on a launch day or for a meeting with your whole team. Production releases are scheduled outside your busiest business hours.",
  },
  {
    question: "What support do we get after launch?",
    answer: `Every project includes five months of free maintenance after launch: bug fixes, security and dependency updates, backups, monitoring and small changes. After that, maintenance continues from ${both("Maintenance")}, or you contact us only when you need something. We agree response times in writing, such as same-day replies to issues raised within our working hours, but we do not run a 24-hour operations centre. If your system needs round-the-clock on-call support, we will say so early and help you plan for it.`,
  },
  {
    question: "Can an IT subsidy pay for our project?",
    answer: "Not with us. Japan's IT adoption subsidy and similar programmes require registered IT support providers and registered tools, and we are not registered, so our work is not eligible. Some other subsidies may cover system costs under their own rules, but whether an overseas provider qualifies depends on the programme. Check with the programme office or your local chamber of commerce before choosing any vendor. If a subsidy is essential to your budget, a registered Japanese provider is the practical route.",
  },
  {
    question: "How long does a custom software project take?",
    answer: "It depends on scope, but a focused first version is usually a matter of weeks rather than months, and the timeline table on this page shows typical ranges for each project type. The most common causes of delay in Japan are internal approvals involving several people, requirements that change after sign-off, slow access to existing systems and data, and translation of screen text. We plan for those in the schedule and release in stages, so your team can start using the core features before everything is finished.",
  },
  {
    question: "How do we start a software project with you?",
    answer: "Send a WhatsApp message or email in English describing your business, the problem you want to solve, the tools you use today and any deadline. Screenshots of current spreadsheets or systems help a great deal. We reply with questions, then send a written scope, a timeline and a quote in USD or JPY, usually within two working days. Nothing is billed until you approve the scope, and the first payment only starts the work described in it.",
  },
];

