  {
    id: "why-outsource",
    heading: "Why Japanese companies outsource software: the IT talent shortage and DX",
    paragraphs: [
      "Japan has had a structural shortage of IT engineers for years. The Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry estimated that the country could be short of up to 790,000 IT workers by 2030. The gap is felt most outside the big cities, where small and mid-sized companies cannot match Tokyo salaries or attract experienced engineers at all. Many firms therefore have one person who looks after the network, the PCs and the accounting software, and nobody who can build or maintain a system.",
      `The government has also pushed digital transformation (${ja("DX")}) for years. METI's DX Report of September 2018 coined the phrase "the 2025 digital cliff" (${ja("2025年の崖")}), warning that if companies could not modernise ageing, heavily customised and poorly documented legacy systems, Japan could face economic losses of up to ¥12 trillion a year from 2025. For most SMEs the pressure is more immediate: fewer staff to re-type data, customers who expect online ordering and booking, and legal changes to invoices and electronic records that paper processes handle badly.`,
      "For a small company, the practical situation is usually one of three. Work runs on Excel files and email because no system fits. A system built years ago by a vendor that has since stopped supporting it still runs critical processes. Or a SaaS tool was bought but never connected to anything else, so staff copy data between tools by hand. Each is fixable without a large project, and outsourcing is often the only realistic way to do it.",
      "Outsourcing does not remove the need for ownership inside your company. The projects that go well have one person who decides priorities, knows the current process and can test the result. We supply the engineering; that person supplies the knowledge of your business. When nobody inside can take that role, we recommend starting with a smaller first release rather than a large system nobody owns.",
