    paragraphs: [
      `Offshore development (${ja("オフショア開発")}) is a familiar model in Japan. Vendors in Vietnam, China, the Philippines, India, Bangladesh and elsewhere build software for Japanese clients, usually through a Japanese sales office and a bilingual bridge engineer who handles communication. The attraction is the same everywhere: engineering capacity that is hard to hire locally, at a lower cost. In the 2025 edition of the ${ja("オフショア開発白書")} (Offshore Development White Paper), published by the Japanese matching site ${ja("オフショア開発.com")}, Vietnam was by far the most common destination, followed by China and India.`,
      "India is a less common choice for Japanese SMEs, and the main reason is language. Indian engineering teams usually work in English, while many Japanese companies want to specify, review and discuss in Japanese. Some offshore firms in other countries train Japanese-speaking engineers for exactly this reason. We do not pretend to solve that with a translation layer. We recommend an India team, and us, mainly when your side already has someone who is comfortable working in English.",
      `<strong>The bridge SE question.</strong> On large offshore projects a bridge SE (${ja("ブリッジSE")}) translates requirements, reviews deliverables and manages the offshore team. That role costs money and adds a layer between the people who know the business and the people who write the code. On a small or mid-sized project, a bilingual contact on your side does the same job better: someone who reads the scope, relays feedback from colleagues and approves releases. We make that person's job easy with short English messages, annotated screenshots, a staging environment and a running decision log.`,
      "<strong>What an India team brings.</strong> India has a very large software engineering workforce with strong English and long experience of cloud platforms, data engineering and AI, built through work for clients in the United States, Europe and Australia. For a Japanese company that already runs partly in English, or a foreign subsidiary in Japan, that combination is often a better fit than a Japanese-speaking offshore team whose English is limited.",
      "<strong>Making it work.</strong> Write the scope in English and keep it short; keep Japanese screen text in a separate sheet; release to staging in small steps so misunderstandings surface within days, not months; and agree which person on each side makes decisions. Offshore projects that fail usually follow the same pattern, long silent periods followed by one big delivery that misses the point, and staged releases remove most of that risk.",
