  {
    id: "contracts",
    heading: "Ukeoi, jun-inin and SES: Japanese contract types and how our work maps to them",
    paragraphs: [
      "Japanese software contracts usually fall into a few types, and your legal or purchasing team will ask which one applies. This is a practical summary for planning, not legal advice.",
      `<strong>Ukeoi (${ja("請負契約")}), a contract for work.</strong> The vendor promises to complete a defined deliverable, and payment is tied to that result. Most fixed-price system builds are contracted this way. The vendor carries the risk of finishing the agreed scope, which is why Japanese vendors invest so much effort in requirements before signing: anything not written down becomes a dispute about scope later.`,
      `<strong>Jun-inin (${ja("準委任契約")}), a quasi-mandate.</strong> The vendor promises to perform work with professional care rather than to deliver a specific result, and is usually paid for time or a monthly fee. Requirements definition, consulting, maintenance and agile development are often contracted this way, because the output cannot be fully specified in advance. Since the 2020 Civil Code revision, a quasi-mandate can also be paid on delivery of a result (${ja("成果完成型")}), which some companies use for phases with a clear output but an open method.`,
      `<strong>SES (system engineering service).</strong> A common Japanese arrangement in which a vendor supplies engineers, often to work at the client's site, under a quasi-mandate contract. Because it is not worker dispatch, the client is generally not supposed to direct those engineers directly; doing so risks being treated as disguised contracting (${ja("偽装請負")}). We do not offer SES and do not place engineers under a client's direct instruction.`,
      "<strong>How our projects map.</strong> New builds are fixed-scope: a written scope, a fixed price, milestone payments and acceptance against agreed criteria, which is close to ukeoi. Maintenance and continuous improvement are a monthly service, closer to jun-inin, and can be stopped with notice. Discovery work on an unclear problem can be a small fixed-price phase whose output is the scope document itself. Model contracts for system development have been published for the Japanese IT industry, and your team is welcome to use one as the starting point, with the Japanese-law wording checked by your own adviser.",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "requirements-quality",
    heading: "Requirements, testing and acceptance: meeting Japanese quality expectations",
    paragraphs: [
      `Japanese system development traditionally follows a clear sequence: requirements definition (${ja("要件定義")}), basic design (${ja("基本設計")}), detailed design (${ja("詳細設計")}), coding, then unit, integration and system testing, and finally the client's acceptance testing and formal acceptance (${ja("検収")}). Each phase ends with documents the client reviews. The model is heavy, but the expectation behind it is reasonable: nothing should reach production that has not been checked against something written down.`,
      "<strong>How we adapt it.</strong> We keep the discipline and drop the weight. Requirements become a short English scope with a screen list, user roles, data items, business rules and acceptance criteria. Design becomes screen sketches and a data model reviewed together in a call. Instead of one delivery at the end, features are released to staging in small batches, so your team checks real screens against the scope every week.",
      `<strong>Test specifications.</strong> Japanese clients often expect a test specification (${ja("テスト仕様書")}): a table of test cases with steps, expected results, actual results and a pass or fail mark. We write one for the main workflows and for the business rules that matter most, such as tax rounding, closing dates and permission checks, and we add automated tests for calculations and integrations so the same checks run on every future change.`,
      "<strong>Acceptance.</strong> Before release, your team tests on staging for an agreed period using real scenarios, ideally with anonymised copies of real data. Issues go into one shared list with screenshots and are classified as defects against the scope or as new requests, then fixed or quoted accordingly. Sign-off happens when the agreed criteria pass, and it triggers the final milestone payment.",
      `<strong>Defects after delivery.</strong> Since the revision of Japan's Civil Code in 2020, the old concept of warranty against defects (${ja("瑕疵担保責任")}) has been replaced by liability for non-conformity with the contract (${ja("契約不適合責任")}), under which a client can generally ask for repair, a price reduction, damages or termination when a deliverable does not match the contract. Under the default rules the client must notify the vendor within one year of discovering the problem, but contracts commonly set their own period and limits, so this should be written into your contract and reviewed by your adviser. In practice, anything that does not match the agreed scope is fixed at no cost, and the five free months of maintenance after launch cover fixes and small changes.`,
    ],
