  {
    id: "japan-specifics",
    heading: "Japanese business rules that custom software has to get right",
    paragraphs: [
      "Most software problems in Japan are not technical. They come from business conventions that foreign templates and packages ignore. These are the ones we plan for from the first scope document.",
    ],
    list: [
      `<strong>Closing dates and payment terms.</strong> B2B billing usually runs on a monthly closing date (${ja("締め日")}) with terms such as closing at month end and paying at the end of the following month (${ja("月末締め翌月末払い")}). Customer-specific closing dates, payment terms and consolidated invoices covering many deliveries must be modelled in the data from the start, not bolted on later.`,
      "<strong>Consumption tax and rounding.</strong> The standard 10 percent and reduced 8 percent rates must be tracked per line, and on a qualified invoice the tax is rounded once per rate per invoice rather than line by line. Getting this wrong produces one-yen differences that accountants notice immediately.",
      "<strong>Dates and fiscal years.</strong> Many companies run an April-to-March fiscal year, some documents and older customers expect Japanese era years such as Reiwa next to the Western year, and business-day calculations need Japanese national holidays plus company closures around Golden Week, Obon and the New Year.",
      `<strong>Bank transfer files.</strong> Bulk supplier payments and payroll are often uploaded to banks as files in the Zengin format (${ja("全銀フォーマット")}), a fixed-length layout in which account names are written in half-width katakana. Systems that pay suppliers need to generate it exactly.`,
      `<strong>Names, readings and addresses.</strong> Family name first, a katakana reading field (${ja("フリガナ")}) for sorting and phone calls, postal-code address lookup, and normalisation of full-width and half-width characters so that searches and duplicate checks actually work.`,
      `<strong>Forms that print.</strong> Quotations, delivery notes and invoices (${ja("帳票")}) often have to match an established layout, sometimes with the company seal image, and are sent as PDF. Layout work belongs in the scope, not in the last week.`,
      `<strong>Approval routes.</strong> Purchases, discounts and contracts often need a multi-step internal approval (${ja("稟議")}), with substitutes when a manager is away. We build routes that follow your internal rules and keep a complete audit trail.`,
      "<strong>CSV and Excel.</strong> Staff open exports in Excel on Japanese Windows, which reads plain CSV files in the Japanese Shift_JIS encoding by default. We export UTF-8 with a byte order mark or native Excel files so Japanese text never turns into garbled characters.",
    ],
