  {
    id: "excel-kintone",
    heading: "Replacing Excel and outgrown kintone apps",
    paragraphs: [
      "Excel still runs a great deal of day-to-day work in Japanese companies: quotations, order books, stock lists, shift rosters and sales reports passed around by email or kept on a shared drive. It works until two people edit different copies, a formula breaks without anyone noticing, or the one person who understands the macros moves on.",
      "kintone, Cybozu's no-code business app platform, is a common next step, and a good one for many teams. Problems start when an app grows beyond what it was designed for: dozens of lookup fields between apps, plugins stacked on plugins, JavaScript customisations nobody documented, and reports that still need an export to Excel. Performance, permissions and data integrity become harder to manage with every change.",
      "<strong>Signs it is time for a custom system:</strong> the same data is entered in two places; month-end closing depends on one person's spreadsheet; customers or suppliers need access that your current tool cannot give safely; calculations such as pricing, commissions or stock valuation are too complex for formulas; or licence costs grow with every user who only needs to look at data.",
      "<strong>How we approach it.</strong> We collect the current files and screens, list every field and rule, and agree which ones still matter. The new system gets a proper relational data model, user roles, a history of who changed what, and exports in the formats staff rely on. Existing data is cleaned and migrated in rehearsals before the switch. Where kintone works well as an entry screen, we can keep it and move only the heavy processing, customer access or reporting into a custom service connected through the kintone REST API.",
      `A focused replacement like this fits our custom web app starting price of ${both("Custom web app")}; the quote depends on how many workflows, roles and reports it covers.`,
