  {
    id: "legacy-cloud",
    heading: "Legacy modernisation and cloud migration in the Tokyo and Osaka regions",
    paragraphs: [
      "Many Japanese companies still run important systems on an on-premises server in the office or a data centre: an Access database, an Excel VBA tool, a Visual Basic client application, or a PHP or Java web system written ten or twenty years ago. The original vendor may have closed or stopped supporting it, the server's operating system may be out of support, and the source code may be incomplete or undocumented.",
      "<strong>Staged modernisation.</strong> We avoid big-bang rewrites. First we document what the system actually does, including rules found only in the code or in staff habits. Then we rebuild in slices, one workflow at a time, with the old and new systems running side by side and data kept in sync until each slice is proven. The riskiest part, usually the data, is rehearsed more than once before the final switch.",
      "<strong>Japanese data conversion.</strong> Legacy Japanese data brings specific work: text stored in Shift_JIS or CP932 converted to UTF-8, machine-dependent characters such as circled numbers and single-character company abbreviations, half-width katakana in names and addresses, custom kanji (gaiji) used in people's names, Japanese era dates stored as text, and codes whose meaning lives only in someone's memory. We write conversion scripts with checks that count and compare records, so nothing disappears quietly.",
      "<strong>Cloud architecture in Japan.</strong> AWS (Tokyo ap-northeast-1 and Osaka ap-northeast-3), Google Cloud (Tokyo asia-northeast1 and Osaka asia-northeast2) and Microsoft Azure (Japan East and Japan West) all operate regions in Japan. A common design runs production in Tokyo and keeps backups, and for critical systems a standby environment, in Osaka, so an earthquake or regional outage in one area does not stop the business. The account is opened in your company's name, which keeps billing, data and access under your control and makes data residency in Japan straightforward to document.",
      "<strong>DevOps.</strong> Infrastructure is defined as code so it can be reviewed and rebuilt, deployments run through an automated pipeline with tests, and monitoring, log retention, cost alerts and backup restore tests are set up before launch. Santosh, who handles AWS and deployment on our team, documents the setup in English so the next engineer can take it over without guesswork.",
